"# Writing shared knowledge\n\nSave reusable facts in the knowledge base, not transient task progress.\n\nWrite when you discover a verified procedure, a repeated failure and its fix, or a stable fact useful to other agents. Do not store secrets, guesses, or conversation logs. Write only to a knowledge base you are allowed to change; the factory aos knowledge base is owned by humans.\n\nBefore writing, call `memory` with `{\"op\":\"find\",\"query\":\"your topic\"}`. Read what matches. Update an existing entry instead of adding a duplicate.\n\nCall `memory_write` to change the knowledge base:\n\n- add a folder first if `memory` read does not list it yet: `{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"notes/\",\"description\":\"Notes about people\"}`.\n- add: `{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"notes/amy.json\",\"value\":\"Amy likes tea.\",\"description\":\"Who Amy is\"}`. `value` and `description` are both required; the description is the one line shown in listings. Give plain text as a plain string, not JSON inside a string.\n- replace the content: `{\"op\":\"set\",\"path\":\"notes/amy.json\",\"value\":\"Amy likes coffee.\"}`\n- remove: `{\"op\":\"rm\",\"path\":\"notes/amy.json\"}`\n\nKeep each text at most 2 KB; split a larger topic into several keys or files. Use a short title, a one-sentence summary, and concrete examples.\n\nThere is no lock. If two agents write the same place at the same time, the later write overwrites the earlier one. So before `set`, `read` the path again and change the text you just read.\n\nIf `memory_write` is not registered, ask a human to register `lib/agent/memory_write` the same way as `memory`, with the same `AOS_MEMORY` in its env (the knowledge base root it writes to). You can also add it yourself with `self_tools`: see `me.json#/self_tools`.\n"
